                <pb facs="0924" n="780" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg780"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.9331/133: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Nanking</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Price</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-01-28T22:00:00+08:00">January 28, 1929—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 29—2:39 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>From Perkins: Reference Department’s telegram of January 24, 11 a.m. This
                    afternoon, in a personal conversation with the Chinese Minister for Foreign
                    Affairs, I made a full presentation of the Department’s views regarding an
                    exchange of notes and emphasized the desire of the Department for a conclusion
                    as soon as possible of this matter. Nevertheless, Dr. Wang stated he could not
                    accept the precise phraseology used by the Department. As soon as his views are
                    received in writing, I shall telegraph them. This has been repeated to
                    Peking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Price</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
